{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And to serve Him with all your heart.</b> Our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: which is the service that is in the heart? You must say — this is prayer. And thus they said, of blessed memory, in Berakhot: 'this service' is prayer, as it is said of Daniel (Daniel 6:17): 'the Elohim whom you serve (פלח) continually,' and was there any divine service in Babylon? Rather, it was called service because he was praying, as it says (ibid.): 'and windows were open to him,' and thus David, peace be upon him, says (Psalms 141:2): 'Let my prayer be set forth as incense before You.' And they said, of blessed memory: the prayers were instituted as corresponding to the daily offerings (תמידין)." "<b>And you need</b> to know that the power of prayer is great, even to change nature, and to be delivered from danger, and to annul what has been decreed. To change nature — from Isaac, as it is said (Genesis 25:21): 'And Isaac entreated HaShem opposite his wife,' and our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: why were the matriarchs barren? Because the Holy One, blessed be He, desires the prayers of the righteous. They explained to us here that barrenness did not come upon the matriarchs except because of prayer, and when they prayed in this matter, nature was changed in them. To be delivered from danger — from what is written (Psalms 107:25–26): 'They that go down to the sea in ships,' and so forth, 'For He commanded and raised the stormy wind, which lifted up its waves; they mounted up to heaven, they went down to the deeps, their soul melted in evil trouble, and they cried to HaShem in their distress,' and after it is written: 'He made the storm a calm, and their waves were still' — from here that prayer protects against danger. To annul what has been decreed — from Hezekiah, to whom the Holy One, blessed be He, added fifteen years through the power of prayer, as it is said (Isaiah 38:5): 'I have heard your prayer, I have seen your tears; behold, I will add to your days fifteen years,' and the word 'I will add' is a sign that his time was fixed and decreed, and his prayer availed him to annul what was decreed. And even what the Blessed Name — may He be exalted — decreed upon him, it is possible for it to be annulled through the power of prayer; for thus Hezekiah said to Isaiah son of Amoz: 'Your prophecy is finished — go out; thus I have received from the house of my father's father: even if a sword is laid upon a person's neck, let him not refrain from mercy,' as it is said (Job 13:15): 'Though He slay me, I will hope in Him.' He informed us here that prayer is above prophecy, that is, above the place from which the effluxes of prophecy issue to the prophets. And you will also need to know that from the days of Moses until the Men of the Great Assembly, prayer in Israel was not arranged in a single fixed order for all, but each person would pray and compose his own expression according to his knowledge and wisdom, until the Men of the Great Assembly came and instituted this prayer of Eighteen, so that it would be ordered in everyone's mouth; and therefore they composed it in simple, understood language, so that thoughts would not be confused in understanding the language, and so that all Israel would be equal in it, both the wise and the foolish. But they instituted it with great intention and with extraordinary providence, in that it is arranged as three blessings of praise before the prayer — and they are Fathers, Mightinesses, and Sanctification of the Name — and three after it — and they are Service, Thanksgiving, and the Priestly Blessing — and they arranged for us in the middle twelve blessings, which comprise all the needs of a person. And all this was not by chance but through necessary and required investigation. And if you examine the Great Name, the distinctive one, which is divided into twelve permutations, and the sign for it is 'זה שמי לעלם' (Exodus 3:15), you will find three first names corresponding to Fathers, Mightinesses, and Sanctification of the Name; three last names corresponding to Service, Thanksgiving, and the Priestly Blessing; six middle names corresponding to six blessings, which are: 'You grant understanding,' 'Restore us,' 'Forgive us,' 'Redemption,' 'Healing,' and 'Sustenance.' And you must add front and back in the six remaining ones, which are: Ingathering of exiles, Justice, Over the righteous, Building (of Jerusalem), Kingdom, and Prayer (that is, 'Hear our voice'). And the blessing of the heretics has no counterpart in this Name, because it corresponds to the attribute of judgment; and as for the Glory of the harsh ones, which is in the psalm (Psalms 29:1): 'Ascribe to HaShem, O sons of elim' — it is an addition to the eighteen mentions of the Name in it; and they instituted it so that the attribute of judgment would be drawn against it and against the enemies, and included further in this is the subjugation of the evil impulse in a person, which is included among the enemies and which partakes of the attribute of judgment." "<b>And you,</b> understand this little and see how these Sages, of blessed memory, did with intention: six blessings for the needs of human beings, and six for the restoration of the crown to its former state and the kingdom of the house of David to its place, and with the blessing of the heretics they are thirteen — and this corresponds to the thirteen attributes, to hint that all the good of the world and its fullness, whether in the needs of human beings or in the repair of the world through the kingdom of Shaddai — all flows from the attributes. And upon you is it to pray to the Cause of causes, may He be blessed, who influences His power into the attributes, in the manner of what is written (Deuteronomy 4:7): 'who has Elohim near to Him' — He says that there is no nation for whom the attributes are near to Him as for this one, and when? 'In all our calling to Him' — to the Cause of causes, may He be exalted; and it says 'to Him' to refer to the attributes; and I have already mentioned this."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {1 "The verse cited as Daniel 6:17 follows the chapter division in the Hebrew Bible (Christian Daniel 6:11 and 6:10). The phrase 'זה שמי לעלם' is from Exodus 3:15. The parenthetical identification of six middle blessing-names and six remaining ones is supplied from the known list of the Eighteen Benedictions to resolve the abbreviated Hebrew references. 'The Glory of the harsh ones' (כבוד הרעים) likely refers to the additional nineteenth blessing; the text is obscure and rendered hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:47:47.700730456Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}